Chunk: 1  Introduction  In the modern world, plastics and synthetic polymers have been integrated into every aspect of our daily lives (  Potnuri et al., 2022b ). Every piece of synthetic plastic ever created still exists in some form on Earth, which now constitutes a major environmental issue ( Geyer et al., 2017 ). The adaptability and convenient qualities of synthetic polymers have led to their widespread use. However, there is an urgent need for new, sustainable methods to combat plastic pollution, given that current methods have proven ineffective against the accumulation of plastic waste ( Sridevi et al., 2022 ). Plastic biodegradation has emerged as an environmentally sound and long-term solution that also facilitates recycling ( Potnuri et al., 2022c ; Sangiorgio et al., 2021 ). Despite the problems surrounding the disposal of plastics, they offer opportunities to reshape their role in the context of circular usage and recycling. Additionally, they are an essential and replaceable
