Chunk: 4.4  Refining additional enzyme functionalities  Efforts to improve plastic-degrading enzymes have been conducted in other areas, including lowering the effects of product inhibition, allowing for catalytic promiscuity in the enzyme, and producing versatile biocatalysts (  Fig. 9 ). First, enzyme activity can be inhibited by intermediates or products of plastic degradation ( Gro\u03b2 et al., 2017 ), and this inhibition can be minimized by changing the architecture of an active site. For example, the G62A mutation in the substrate binding groove of TfCut2 resulted in a 5.5-fold decrease in the binding constant for the inhibitory hydrolysis product, mono (2-hydroxyethyl) terephthalate (MHET), thus resulting in improved PET degradation ( Ren et al., 2016 ). Additionally, fusion with an additional enzyme that can break down the intermediate molecules has been shown to reduce product inhibition ( Barth et al., 2016 ). Enabling catalytic promiscuity can increase the capacity of
